    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports Is a Tongue Twister, Weighs Less but Offers More\nNew Vertical Powertrain Center for the Valo eVTOL Unveiled in Bristol\n7 photos\nPhoto: Vertical Aerospace\nVertical Aerospace, one of the most important AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) companies in Europe right now, is celebrating another milestone, with construction completed on its brand-new Vertical Powertrain Center in Bristol. The facility isn’t operational yet, but it aims to boost battery production for electric aircraft considerably.\nThe eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) scene in Europe is looking pretty dim, sadly, with a lot fewer manufacturing still standing and going strong. The tragic fate of Germany’s Volocopter, one of the most highly-anticipated air taxis made in Europe, highlighted once more the financial fragility of AAM startups in an industry that is still in its early beginnings.\nVertical Aerospace has remained one of the very few European eVTOL makers with solid plans to actually produce their aircraft on the Old Continent. At the same time as developing and upgrading its flagship aircraft, named Valo, Vertical has also worked steadily on building a robust manufacturing infrastructure in support of future commercial operations.\nThe Vertical Energy Center (VEC) opened in 2023, covering 15,000 square feet. This is where the batteries that were used for Vertical’s piloted flights over the past three years have been designed, tested, and produced. This year, VEC reached a new level, by producing the first all-electric Valo battery on its upgraded assembly line.\nThere’s also the Aircraft Assembly Center, officially opened at Cotswold Airport in September. Now, Vertical has announced that a new center, the Vertical Powertrain Center, is finalized and ready for equipment installation.\nThis new center, scheduled to kick off operations by next year, is located next to VEC in Bristol, and as its name suggests, will be exclusively dedicated to electric and hybrid-electric powertrain engineering, system integration, and battery testing.\nThe Powertrain Center is twice as big as VEC, unfolding over 30,000 square feet, and Vertical expects it will triple battery-production capacity in the near future. With a Valo eVTOL expected to require approximately 20 battery packs throughout its life cycle, this new Center is also expected to generate income for the company beyond individual aircraft sales.\nMore than that, the Center’s production will expand beyond eVTOL operations, through battery recycling and other re-use applications that will support other sectors in aerospace and mobility.\nVertical’s plans sound promising, but he only question left is where will it manufacture its actual aircraft? The location of its first full-scale aircraft production site is yet to be unveiled, but Vertical confirmed plans to announce it before the end of this year, so stay tuned.\nVertical’s flagship aircraft, Valo, is a four-passenger eVTOL primarily aimed at air taxi operations. Commercially, the UK-based company has secured more than 1,500 pre-orders for Valo, but is also working on a hybrid-electric version with better range, that could expand to multiple applications beyond air taxi service.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVertical Aerospace Vertical Powertrain Center Valo eVTOL evtol electric aircraft\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT A Breath of Fresh Air: New Yachting Design House Kunsen Drops Its K37 Cabrio and Tender Concepts",
